ce:section.ce:para:
The goal of this study was to determine the potential environmental impacts of the UASB reactor coupled with HRAPs for wastewater treatment and bioenergy recovery in small communities in Brazil. This configuration was compared to other low-cost and nature-based technologies that have already been established in the country. Overall, four alternatives were compared:

                                 1)

UASB reactor followed by a trickling filter (scenario UASB+TKF);


                                 2)

UASB reactor followed by HRAPs (scenario UASB+ HRAP);


                                 3)

UASB reactor followed by polishing ponds (scenario UASB+PP);


                                 4)

UASB reactor followed by horizontal sub-surface flow constructed wetlands (scenario UASB+CW).

The functional unit (FU) for this study was set as 1 m3 of treated water, since the main function of the solutions proposed is to treat wastewater. The cradle-to-grave boundaries included systems construction, operation and maintenance of the full-scale systems over a 20-years period (Arashiro et al., 2018
                           ; 
                           Garfí et al., 2017
                           ; 
                           Pérez-López et al., 2017). Input and output flows of materials (i.e. construction materials) and energy resources (heat and electricity) were systematically studied for all scenarios. Direct greenhouse gas (GHG) emissions and NH4
                           + volatilization associated with wastewater treatment were also included in the boundaries (Søvik et al., 2006). Direct emissions to water were also considered, since treated wastewater is discharged into a water body (river). Regarding the sludge produced in all scenarios, transportation of dewatered sludge to the landfill was also taken into account (20 km - average distance to the landfill in Brazil). The end-of-life of infrastructures and equipment were neglected, since the impact would be marginal compared to the overall impact (Arashiro et al., 2018; Garfí et al., 2017; Pérez-López et al., 2017). Since the studied scenarios would generate by-products (i.e. biogas), the system expansion method was used following the ISO guidelines (Guinée, 2006). In this method, by-products are supposed to avoid the production of conventional products. Thus, the impacts associated with the production of conventional products were withdrawn from the overall impact of the systems (Arashiro et al., 2018; Sfez et al., 2015; ISO, 2006). Specifically, the avoided impacts of using heat and electricity produced by biogas cogeneration instead of heat from natural gas and electricity from the grid were considered.

In all scenarios, inventory data regarding construction materials and systems operation were based on the detailed engineering designs performed in the frame of this study (Table 2
                           ). As mentioned above, treated effluents characteristics were estimated considering the removal efficiencies and experimental results of previous studies. In the case of the trickling filter and constructed wetlands scenarios (scenarios UASB+TKF and UASB+CW, respectively) direct CH4 and N2O emissions were estimated using emission factors from the literature (Gutierrez, 2014). NH4
                           + volatilization in the HRAPs and polishing ponds scenarios (scenarios UASB+HRAP and UASB+PP, respectively) was estimated through nitrogen mass balance (De Assunção and Von Sperling, 2013; Vassalle et al., 2020). Since heavy metals were not detected in the influent wastewater, they were not considered in any scenario (Dornelas et al., 2009; Vassalle et al., 2020). As mentioned above, in order to estimate electricity and heat production from biogas cogeneration, biogas production obtained in pilot-scale experiments was taken into account (Vassalle et al., 2020). Background data (i.e. data of construction materials, chemicals, energy production, transportation, sludge landfilling) were obtained from the Ecoinvent 3 database (Moreno Ruiz et al., 2020; Weidema et al., 2013).
